Week 2 – Data Preparation and Preprocessing
 
1. Introduction
Week 2 focuses on preparing the logistics dataset for reliable downstream analytics. Data cleaning is essential because inaccurate values, inconsistent data types, duplicate records, missing information, and extreme observations can produce misleading KPIs and unreliable predictive models. The objective is to establish a repeatable preprocessing pipeline that converts the raw logistics data into analysis-ready tables while preserving legitimate business information.
2. Data Collection Simulation and Dataset Characteristics
The project continues to use the relational logistics database introduced during Week 1. It contains 14 connected tables representing customers, drivers, trucks, trailers, facilities, routes, loads, trips, fuel purchases, maintenance records, delivery events, safety incidents, driver monthly metrics, and truck utilization metrics.
Table	Rows	Columns
customers	200	8
drivers	150	12
trucks	120	11
trailers	180	9
facilities	50	9
routes	58	9
loads	85,410	12
trips	85,410	12
fuel_purchases	196,442	11
maintenance_records	2,920	12
delivery_events	170,820	11
safety_incidents	170	15
driver_monthly_metrics	4,464	9
truck_utilization_metrics	3,312	10
The raw CSV files are analyzed locally in Python and kept separate from processed outputs so the original source data remains unchanged.
3. Data Cleaning Methodology
•	Missing values were examined and treated according to business meaning rather than applying one rule to every field.
•	Exact duplicate rows were checked across the available tables.
•	Numerical trip fields were validated for physically impossible values.
•	Date and timestamp fields were converted to Pandas datetime types.
•	The IQR method was used to identify statistical outliers.
•	Selected numerical variables were standardized with StandardScaler for future scale-sensitive analysis.
4. Missing-Value Analysis
The Drivers table contained 124 missing values in termination_date. These values were retained rather than statistically imputed because a missing termination date can represent an active driver. Replacing the values with a mean, median, or artificial date would introduce incorrect business information.
for name, df in datasets.items():
    missing = df.isnull().sum()
    missing = missing[missing > 0]
    if len(missing) > 0:
        print(name.upper())
        print(missing)
5. Duplicate Analysis
Exact duplicate rows were checked across the datasets. The Week 1 quality assessment found no duplicate rows in the checked tables, so no duplicate-based deletion was required. This prevents legitimate records from being removed merely because some business fields are similar.
for name, df in datasets.items():
    print(name, "duplicates:", df.duplicated().sum())
6. Data-Type and Date Preprocessing
Date and timestamp fields were converted to datetime values. This enables chronological comparisons, duration calculations, time-based aggregation, and future seasonal analysis.
trips = datasets["trips"].copy()
delivery = datasets["delivery_events"].copy()

trips["dispatch_date"] = pd.to_datetime(
    trips["dispatch_date"], errors="coerce"
)

delivery["scheduled_datetime"] = pd.to_datetime(
    delivery["scheduled_datetime"], errors="coerce"
)

delivery["actual_datetime"] = pd.to_datetime(
    delivery["actual_datetime"], errors="coerce"
)
7. Invalid-Value Validation
The Trips table was checked for negative distance, duration, fuel consumption, and idle time, as well as non-positive MPG. The actual validation produced zero invalid values for all five checks.
Validation	Invalid Count
Negative distance	0
Negative duration	0
Negative fuel	0
Invalid MPG (≤ 0)	0
Negative idle time	0
print("Negative distance:", (trips["actual_distance_miles"] < 0).sum())
print("Negative duration:", (trips["actual_duration_hours"] < 0).sum())
print("Negative fuel:", (trips["fuel_gallons_used"] < 0).sum())
print("Invalid MPG:", (trips["average_mpg"] <= 0).sum())
print("Negative idle time:", (trips["idle_time_hours"] < 0).sum())
8. Outlier Detection Using IQR
Outliers were investigated using the Interquartile Range (IQR) method. For each variable, Q1 and Q3 were calculated and the acceptable range was defined as Q1 − 1.5×IQR to Q3 + 1.5×IQR. Observations outside that range were counted.
def detect_outliers_iqr(df, column):
    Q1 = df[column].quantile(0.25)
    Q3 = df[column].quantile(0.75)
    IQR = Q3 - Q1
    lower = Q1 - 1.5 * IQR
    upper = Q3 + 1.5 * IQR
    outliers = df[(df[column] < lower) | (df[column] > upper)]
    return outliers, lower, upper
Variable	Lower Bound	Upper Bound	Outliers
actual_distance_miles	-1428.00	4292.00	0
actual_duration_hours	-23.85	73.35	0
fuel_gallons_used	-210.10	648.30	0
average_mpg	4.50	8.50	0
idle_time_hours	-3.00	17.00	0
The IQR procedure identified zero statistical outliers in each selected trip variable. Therefore, no observations were removed or capped. This is appropriate because unusual logistics observations can be legitimate long-distance or high-duration trips.
9. Outlier Visualization
Boxplots were created for actual trip distance, fuel consumption, and idle time to visually inspect the distributions and support the numerical IQR results. The plots form part of the Week 2 notebook evidence.
10. Numerical Normalization
StandardScaler was applied to actual distance, actual duration, fuel consumed, average MPG, and idle time. Standardization places variables on a comparable scale and is particularly useful for future algorithms such as K-Means clustering.
from sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler

numeric_features = [
    "actual_distance_miles",
    "actual_duration_hours",
    "fuel_gallons_used",
    "average_mpg",
    "idle_time_hours"
]

trips_clean = trips.copy()
scaler = StandardScaler()

trips_clean[
    [col + "_scaled" for col in numeric_features]
] = scaler.fit_transform(
    trips_clean[numeric_features]
)
Normalization verification produced means of approximately 0.0 and standard deviations of 1.0 for all five scaled features, confirming that the transformation was successful.
